According to fall_through() in trap.c you drop at least
one level and a random distribution farther:

	do {
	    newlevel++;
	} while(!rn2(4) && newlevel < dunlevs_in_dungeon(&u.uz));

One time in four you drop 2 levels, 1-in-16 you drop 3,
1-in-64 you drop 4 and so on in and exponential taper
off.  My memory of falls matches this well enough - Most
of the time the drop is one level, often enough to know
it happens the drop is two levels, I've lost early characters
to getting dropped three levels and not being able to find
the up stairs to retreat out of a crowd of monsters
beyond my current hit points.  I don't recall ever dropping
4 levels.

The test is not stored per hole or trap door.  I've seen my
pet fall through a trap door, jumped in to follow, and ended
up on a different level.

In this sense a hole seems like a better way to descend
rapidly.  Consider a crowned post-Quest valk who maintains
a stash at a reasonably shallow level.  She'll want to use
the Orb of Weight to get to her stash level then descend
as radidly as possible to resume exploration.  Going
stairs to stairs gives a known path that was probably dug
out.  Jumping into a hole can descend farther but it's to a
random location after each drop.


